---
/**
 * The home page's second half: how the port is checked against Graphviz, what does not match
 * yet, and where to go next. Every figure comes from src/data.
 */
import Mark from './Mark.astro';
import SlugLine from './SlugLine.astro';
import { fidelity, fmt, issues, samples, url } from '../lib/data';

const f = fidelity;
const off = f.e2e.corpora
	.map((c) => ({ name: c.name, n: c.total - c.identical }))
	.filter((c) => c.n > 0)
	.sort((a, b) => b.n - a.n);
const stock = f.e2e.stock;
const twopi = f.layout.find((l) => l.name === 'twopi')!;
const lastFuzz = f.fuzz.rounds[f.fuzz.rounds.length - 1];
const portBugs = issues.filter((i) => !i.class).length;
const upstream = issues.length - portBugs;
const live = samples.filter((s) => s.identical).length;
---

<div class="home">
	<section aria-labelledby="proof">
		<h2 id="proof">How we know it matches</h2>
		<p class="intro">
			Every number here is measured, none typed in by hand. The test suite compares graphviz-pure with Graphviz on every change, and it fails if
			a graph that used to match stops matching. Select a number to see the graphs behind it.
		</p>
		<SlugLine />
		<div class="defgrid">
			<p>
				<strong>Byte for byte.</strong> We compare the whole output file with Graphviz's. The only line we skip is the comment that names the
				program. Whitespace, number formatting and element order all have to match.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Against one exact Graphviz.</strong> The reference is Graphviz 16.1.0 measuring text with its built-in font tables. A typical
				install measures text with Pango and your system fonts instead, so its labels land slightly differently, and nothing else matches it
				exactly. <a href={url('verify/#the-c-reference')}>Set up the reference</a>
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>The same math everywhere.</strong> graphviz-pure brings its own math functions and uses them on every machine, Macs
				included, so every build draws the same bytes. To compare like with like, the reference Graphviz runs with those same functions in
				place of the system's math library.{' '}
				<a href={url('porting/libm/')}>Why the math matters</a>
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="not-yet">
		<h2 id="not-yet">What isn't there yet</h2>
		<ul class="left">
			{off.length > 0 && (
				<li>
					<Mark kind="off" />
					<span>
						<strong class="num">{fmt(f.e2e.total - f.e2e.identical)}</strong> test graphs differ from Graphviz given the same math:{' '}
						{off.map((c, i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}{c.name} {c.n}</>)}.
					</span>
				</li>
			)}
			<li>
				<Mark kind="pending" />
				<span>
					<strong>No PDF output yet</strong>, and none of Graphviz's other tools, such as <code>gvpr</code>. graphviz-pure is the{' '}
					<code>dot</code> command and the library behind it. PNG and JPEG are drawn from the SVG, so they have the same layout as Graphviz but
					not the same pixels.
				</span>
			</li>
			<li>
				<Mark kind="upstream" />
				<span>
					<strong class="num">{fmt(stock.libm_only)}</strong> of the {fmt(stock.total)} test graphs come out differently from stock Graphviz on
					a Mac, because Graphviz there uses Apple's math library. Given the same math functions, Graphviz draws them exactly as graphviz-pure
					does, so they are not bugs.
				</span>
			</li>
			<li>
				<Mark kind="upstream" />
				<span>
					<strong class="num">{twopi.total - twopi.exact}</strong> of {twopi.total} twopi layouts hit a tie that Graphviz itself breaks
					differently from one run to the next, and <strong class="num">{f.e2e.unstable.length}</strong> test graphs are left out because
					Graphviz's own output changes between runs.
				</span>
			</li>
			<li>
				<Mark kind="off" />
				<span>
					<strong class="num">{(100 - lastFuzz.all).toFixed(1)}%</strong> of {fmt(lastFuzz.cases)} random graphs still differ, mostly where
					Graphviz gives different answers from run to run or crashes.
				</span>
			</li>
			<li>
				<Mark kind="pending" />
				<span>
					<strong class="num">{issues.length}</strong> known differences: {portBugs} in graphviz-pure and {upstream} in how Graphviz itself
					behaves. <a href={url('whats-left/')}>See them all</a>
				</span>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="explore">
		<h2 id="explore">Explore</h2>
		<ul class="next">
			<li><a href={url('gallery/')}>Gallery</a><span>{samples.length} graphs across all eight engines, Graphviz's drawing printed over graphviz-pure's so any difference shows. Your browser can re-check {live} of them.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('playground/')}>Playground</a><span>Paste your own DOT and see it laid out in this tab. Nothing leaves the page.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('fidelity/')}>Fidelity</a><span>Every comparison with Graphviz, test suite by test suite, and the graphs that differ.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('cross-platform/')}>Cross-platform</a><span>What each operating system and your browser draw, side by side, straight from CI.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('verify/')}>Verify it yourself</a><span>The commands behind every number on this site.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('changelog/')}>Changelog</a><span>What changed in each release.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('porting/dotgen/')}>Porting notes</a><span>How each part of Graphviz's C code maps to Rust, and every known difference.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('api/')}>API</a><span>Reference docs for the Rust crates.</span></li>
		</ul>
	</section>
</div>

<style>
	.intro {
		max-width: 44rem;
		color: var(--key-2);
	}
	.defgrid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
		gap: 1.5rem;
		border-top: 0.5px solid var(--rule-strong);
		margin-top: 2rem;
		padding-top: 1rem;
	}
	.defgrid p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
		line-height: 1.6;
		color: var(--key-2);
	}
	.defgrid strong {
		display: block;
		color: var(--key);
		font-weight: 650;
		margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
	}
	.left {
		list-style: none;
		padding: 0;
		margin: 0;
		display: grid;
		gap: 0;
		border-top: 0.5px solid var(--rule-strong);
	}
	.left li {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1.5rem 1fr;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		padding: 0.75rem 0;
		margin: 0;
		border-bottom: 0.5px solid var(--rule);
		color: var(--key-2);
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
	}
	.left li :global(.mark) {
		padding-top: 0.2rem;
	}
	.left strong {
		color: var(--key);
		font-weight: 650;
	}
	.next {
		list-style: none;
		padding: 0;
		margin: 0;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
		column-gap: 2.5rem;
		border-top: 0.5px solid var(--rule-strong);
	}
	.next li {
		display: grid;
		gap: 0.125rem;
		padding: 0.75rem 0;
		margin: 0;
		border-bottom: 0.5px solid var(--rule);
	}
	.next a {
		font-weight: 650;
		font-stretch: 112%;
		color: var(--register);
		text-decoration: none;
	}
	.next a:hover {
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
	}
	.next span {
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
		color: var(--key-3);
	}
	@media (max-width: 50rem) {
		.defgrid,
		.next {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
	}
</style>
